---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import {
  calloutInfoTree,
  calloutNeutralTree,
  calloutSuccessTree,
  calloutWarningTree,
} from "../../../demos/callout";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;
const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

---

<Base title="Callout" description="Callout: Persistent inline message with tone (including neutral), accessible announcement and React component.">
  <h1>Callout</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Callout is an inline message that remains in the layout for the duration of its condition. Unlike Toast, which is transient and mounted in a floating region, Callout lives in the flow of content. One visual weight, the bordered panel: tone is the only variable, so two Callouts never compete over which one looks more urgent.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It is purely informational: it shows something, it does not run anything. There is no dismiss;
    unlike Toast, a Callout cannot be dismissed, because nothing on the page depends on it going away.
    The one interactive piece it can carry is a recovery action, and the contract narrows it to
    <code>subtle</code> or <code>danger</code> so it never competes with the page's real primary
    action.
  </p>

  <h2>Tones</h2>
  <p>
    The tone decides whether the panel is painted with semantic color. <code>info</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>warning</code> and <code>danger</code> color the message. Only <code>danger</code> is advertised as <code>role="alert"</code> (assertive); the rest use <code>role="status"</code> (polite); it is the minority of cases that truly interrupt, not the component's name, that decides that.
  </p>
  <p>
    All four read a <strong>feedback role</strong>, never the accent: the tone tells what happened, so it cannot change when the brand changes. <code>info</code> only had his own ramp in decision 26 (<code>docs/decisiones/0026-info-es-un-rol-de-feedback-no-el-acento.md</code>); Before, it read <code>accent</code>, and a magenta mark painted each information notice magenta.
  </p>

  <h3>Neutral</h3>
  <p>
    The default: surface and edge, without semantic painting. It exists for when the color should not be the prominent signal, the text already loads the message. Use it in ordinary notices, short confirmations, or any case where painting the “success” or “info” panel would add urgency that the content does not have.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={calloutNeutralTree(t)} label="Callout neutral" />

  <h3>Info, with a title</h3>
  <p>A title next to the icon names the condition instead of only describing it in the first line.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={calloutInfoTree(t)} label="Callout info" />

  <h3>Warning, with a recovery Link</h3>
  <p>The recovery action is a plain <code>Link</code>: a destination, not a command.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={calloutWarningTree(t)} label="Callout warning" />

  <h3>Success, with a subtle Button</h3>
  <p>
    When the action is a <a href="/en/components/button">Button</a>, the slot only accepts
    <code>variant="subtle"</code> or <code>variant="danger"</code>, never <code>primary</code> nor the
    default <code>neutral</code>: a Callout is not the place for the page's main call to action.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={calloutSuccessTree(t)} label="Callout success" />

  <h2>Anatomy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>sk-callout__icon</code> is decorative and only appears when it provides a visual signal.</li>
    <li><code>sk-callout__content</code> groups optional title and description.</li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-callout__actions</code> hosts the optional recovery action that belongs to the
      consumer, never a dismiss: Callout has none.
    </li>
    <li><code>data-tone</code> accepts <code>neutral</code> (default), <code>info</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>warning</code> or <code>danger</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of each example. <code>actions</code> is the only
    point of interactivity: a <code>ReactNode</code> the consumer builds with whichever
    <code>Link</code> or <code>Button</code> (<code>subtle</code>/<code>danger</code>) it needs.
    There is no <code>dismissible</code> or <code>onDismiss</code>; if the message needs to close,
    that is a <a href="/en/components/toast">Toast</a>, not a Callout.
  </p>
</Base>
